<a name="oraclecharsets"/><h1>Character Set Options</h1>

<p>The Oracle protocol module tells clients which character sets to expect, by Oracle character set id.  It doesn't convert any data, so these options should match what the back-end database actually sends.</p>

<p>The <i>charset</i> option sets the database character set.  It defaults to 873 (AL32UTF8), or to 31 (WE8ISO8859P1) on a <i>verifiertype="9i"</i> listener, where it should be set to 873 if the connection's <i>nls_lang</i> is AL32UTF8.</p>

<p>The <i>nationalcharset</i> option sets the national character set.  It defaults to 2000 (AL16UTF16), and rarely needs to be changed.</p>

<p>If a client can't decode a result set, try setting <i>charset</i> to 178 (WE8MSWIN1252), which decodes any byte, though not always to the right character.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">charset</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"178"</font> <font color="#009900">nationalcharset</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"2000"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>Any Oracle character set id may be used.  0, or anything that isn't a number, is the same as leaving the option out.  To look up the ids of a database's own character sets, run:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><font color="#008080">select</font> parameter<font color="#990000">,</font>value<font color="#990000">,</font><b><font color="#000000">nls_charset_id</font></b><font color="#990000">(</font>value<font color="#990000">)</font>
<font color="#008080">from</font> nls_database_parameters
where <font color="#008080">parameter</font> <b><font color="#000000">in</font></b> <font color="#990000">(</font><font color="#FF0000">'NLS_CHARACTERSET'</font><font color="#990000">,</font><font color="#FF0000">'NLS_NCHAR_CHARACTERSET'</font><font color="#990000">);</font></tt></pre>

</blockquote>
